{
  "result": "There is a specific kind of light that only exists in late August, around five thirty in the evening. It is not quite summer anymore, not yet autumn. The sun hangs lower, thicker, pouring itself like warm honey across kitchen counters and dusty sidewalks. I think about this light often because it feels like a memory even while you are living inside it. \n\nAs a child, I believed time moved in straight lines. School to summer to school again. Birthdays ticking forward like a metronome. Now I know it pools and eddies. Some years vanish entirely while a single Tuesday afternoon in 2017 remains perfectly preserved, the smell of pencil shavings and rain on hot pavement never fading. \n\nWe are all archivists without meaning to be. We collect ticket stubs, text messages we cannot delete, the way someone once laughed at our bad joke. My pockets are full of smooth stones I picked up and forgot to throw back. Not valuable, just proof that I was there, that I noticed. \n\nMaybe that is enough. Not to solve time, but to witness it. To sit in the honeyed August light for one more minute and let it hold us before it goes. Outside, cicadas are beginning their slow chorus. A fan turns lazily overhead. Somewhere a dog barks, and a screen door shuts softly. The world continues, ordinary and miraculous at once, asking nothing from us except that we pay attention while we can. That is the quiet work of living.",
